---

<Layout
  title="How To Read Fender Neck Dates: Neck Heel Dating Guide (Preview)"
  description="Read the pencil date or ink stamp on your Fender's neck heel to date the guitar to the month. A step-by-step neck-date guide from a vintage Fender appraiser."
  canonical={PAGE_PATH}
  ogImage={`${SITE_URL}${img("1959-fender-jazzmaster-neck-heel.jpg")}`}
  ogImageAlt="The neck heel of a 1959 Fender Jazzmaster with a pencil date reading 1-59 for January 1959"
  ogType="article"
  noIndex={true}
  structuredData={[articleSchema, videoSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <link slot="head" rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/lite-yt-embed.css" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" />
  <script slot="head" is:inline src="/scripts/lite-yt-embed.js" defer></script>

  <!-- Archetype B header backdrop: solid bands the absolute site Header sits on -->
  <div class="fspot-header-backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__dark"></div>
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__cream"></div>
  </div>

  <p class="fspot-preview-note">Internal preview of a Fender serial guide spoke. Noindexed and not linked. Some sibling spoke links point at planned slugs that do not exist yet.</p>

  <div class="fspot">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="hero" id="top">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <a class="back" href={HUB_HREF}>Back To The Full Fender Serial Guide</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Fender Dating Methods</span>
        <h1>Fender Neck Dates: How To Date A Fender By Its Neck Heel</h1>
        <p class="lead">Someone at Fender wrote or stamped the date on the butt of your neck by hand, then bolted it into the body where nobody would see it again for sixty years. It is the most reliable single thing on a vintage Fender, and it takes four screws to get to.</p>
        <div class="trust">
          <span>Joe Dampt</span><span>12 years buying vintage Fenders full-time</span><span>10,000+ appraised</span>
        </div>
        <figure class="sfig herofig">
          <LicensedImage src={meta("1959-fender-jazzmaster-neck-heel.jpg")} alt="The neck heel of a 1959 Fender Jazzmaster with a pencil date reading 1-59 for January 1959, the neck lifted out of the body" width={1600} loading="eager" />
          <figcaption>The neck heel of a 1959 Fender Jazzmaster, penciled 1-59 for January 1959. The neck is lifted out of the body to reach it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SAFETY WARNING (top of page) -->
    <section class="pt0" aria-label="Safety warning">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="warnbox" role="note">
          <span class="warnbox__head"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"></path><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path></svg>Read This First</span>
          <p>Do not take a vintage instrument apart unless you have done it before. Vintage screws, plastics, and finishes strip and crack easily, and one slip can cost far more than the answer. If you have not done this before, contact me first and I'll help.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- WHAT -->
    <section id="what">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Start Here</span>
        <h2>What A Neck Date Is</h2>
        <p>The neck date is a production date written or stamped on the heel of the neck, the flat end that bolts into the body. A Fender worker marked it by hand as they finished the neck, so it records the actual month that neck was made. Almost every Fender built through the early 1970s has one.</p>
        <p>It's the single most reliable way to date a vintage Fender, because it was applied at the bench, not assigned from an office. The catch is what it dates. The neck date dates the neck, not the whole guitar. The two line up only when the neck is original to the body.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FIND -->
    <section id="find" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Before You Start</span>
        <h2>Where To Find The Neck Date</h2>
        <p>The date is on the butt of the neck heel, the end that sits down in the neck pocket, so you have to take the neck off the body to see it. On a bolt-on Fender that means backing out the neck plate screws and lifting the neck out of the pocket. Support both halves so nothing swings loose, and take a clear photo of the heel before you put it back together.</p>
        <div class="videowrap">
          <lite-youtube videoid={YT_ID} params="rel=0&modestbranding=1&start=114" title={YT_TITLE} style={`background-image:url('${img("fender-neck-heel-video-poster.jpg")}')`}></lite-youtube>
        </div>
        <p class="note-sm">Watch Joe take the neck off and read the heel date. The clip starts right at that step.</p>

        <h3 id="safely">Taking The Neck Off Without Hurting The Guitar</h3>
        <p>Slack the strings right off first. Taking them off entirely is better, because it drops all the forward pull across the joint before anything moves. Work on a padded surface that holds up the body and the neck at the same height, and keep a hand on both once the screws start coming out.</p>
        <p>Use a screwdriver that actually fills the screw head. A number 2 Phillips is usually right, and a loose fit is how you round out a sixty-year-old screw slot. Back the four screws out slowly and evenly rather than running one all the way out first.</p>
        <p>If the neck sits tight in the pocket, and after decades of lacquer many of them do, do not lever it sideways. The pocket walls are thin and that is exactly how they crack. Ease it straight up from the heel instead and let the seal break on its own.</p>
        <p>Faint marks are normal. Old graphite goes silvery and ink can fade almost into the wood, so try raking a light across the heel at a low angle, and a magnifier or a UV light will often pull up a date your eye reads as blank. Photograph the heel before you bolt it back together, so you never have to take it apart twice.</p>
        <p>If you aren't comfortable taking the neck off, don't force it. A stripped screw or a chipped finish costs more than the answer. Get in touch before you take anything apart and I'll help, or read the date for you if you bring the guitar by.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- STEP 1 -->
    <section id="step1">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Format 1</span>
        <h2>Read A Pencil Date</h2>
        <p>On Fenders from the early 1950s into the early 1960s, the date is written in pencil right on the heel. The format is simple: the month, then the year. Sometimes the worker's initials sit next to it.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap compact"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Pencil date</th><th>Reads as</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{pencilRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Pencil date" class="code">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="Reads as" class="yr">{r[1]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="secintro">So 5-57 reads as May 1957. That is the whole method for a penciled neck. The only trap is a faint or half-erased pencil mark, which is why a raking-light photo beats a quick glance in the pocket.</p>

        <h3 id="early-day">Early 1950s Necks Often Carry The Day As Well</h3>
        <p>Month and year is the format you'll meet most, but it isn't the earliest one. On Broadcasters, Nocasters, early Telecasters, and Stratocasters from 1950 into early 1955, the worker frequently wrote the exact day too, so you get something like 6-4-52 or 10-12-54 instead of a bare 6-52. Three numbers on an early neck is normal. It does not mean somebody has written on it since.</p>
        <p>Those early necks often carry initials in front of the date, and on a 1950s Fender that's a detail worth knowing rather than a smudge to ignore. TG is Tadeo Gomez, who shaped necks at Fender through most of the 1950s, and his necks carry a premium with collectors for exactly that reason. XA is Xavier Armenta, seen mostly around 1956. You'll also run into a lone D stamp on necks, and in body neck pockets, from roughly mid-1951 into the mid 1950s. Nobody has ever established what it meant. Inspected, done, dried, checked, a shift marker and a worker code have all been proposed and none of it is settled, so treat it as a period marking and not as a date. It is not the factory refinish mark either. That one is R&amp;R, and it comes a decade later.</p>

        <h3 id="gap-1959">The 1959 Gap, When Fender Stopped Dating Necks</h3>
        <p>There's a window in 1959 that catches people out. Around March or April, close to when the slab rosewood board came in, Fender stopped penciling dates on neck heels. The practice came back in 1960. In between, a genuine, untouched, correct 1959 neck can come out of the pocket with nothing written on it at all.</p>
        <p>The story on the bench is that a customer complained about something profane a worker had penciled on a heel. It's a good story, and it may well be true, but it has never been documented, so treat it as shop legend rather than history. The gap itself is real and is the part that matters.</p>
        <p>The practical upshot: on a suspected 1959, a blank heel is not evidence of a refinish or a swapped neck. Date it from the body date, the pot codes, and the hardware instead. Not every neck in the window is blank either, so a dated late-1959 neck is not a problem in itself.</p>
        <div class="figrow single">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("fender-neck-heel-pencil-date-decoder.jpg")} alt="A Fender neck heel with a pencil date marking in month-year format" width={1280} /><figcaption>A penciled neck heel date. Month first, then the year.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- STEP 2 -->
    <section id="step2" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Format 2</span>
        <h2>Read An Ink Stamp</h2>
        <p>From about 1962, Fender switched to an ink stamp that packs in more. It runs a model code, then the month, the year, and a final letter for the neck width:</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Part of the stamp</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{stampParts.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Part of the stamp">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="What it means">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="Example" class="code">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="secintro"><b>Worked example.</b> 1APR63B reads as April 1963 on a standard B-width neck. Ignore the model code for dating; the month and the year are what you are after, and the letter is just the nut width. Here is the stamp broken down:</p>
        <div class="figrow single">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("fender-neck-heel-ink-stamp-decoder-scaled-1.jpg")} alt="A Fender neck heel ink stamp decoder showing the model code, month, year, and neck width" width={1280} /><figcaption>The ink stamp broken down: a model code, then the month, the year, and a final letter for neck width.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The letter is a spec, not a date.</b> That final letter is the nut width: A narrow, B standard, C wide, D extra wide. It won't date the neck, but it is a quick way to confirm the neck matches the guitar it is bolted to.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- GREEN STAMP -->
    <section id="green">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Format 3</span>
        <h2>The Green Stamp</h2>
        <p>Starting in 1969, CBS Fender added a new neck stamp in green ink, a run of six to eight digits. For a few years it ran alongside the old month-year stamp, so a Fender from 1969 to 1971 might carry the plain date, the green code, or neither. A neck has one or the other, not both.</p>
        <p>The green code reads from the outside in: the first two digits are the model, the next few are a batch number, then a digit or two for the month, one digit for the year, and a letter for the neck width. A Stratocaster starts with 22. For example, 22 384 10 9 B is a Strat, batch 384, October, 1969, standard width.</p>
        <div class="figrow single portrait">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("1969-fender-neck-heel.jpg")} alt="A green ink neck stamp reading 22 447 1198 on a 1969 Fender Stratocaster neck heel" width={1000} /><figcaption>A green stamp on a 1969 Stratocaster neck. It starts with 22, the Stratocaster code.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>Green codes are the cryptic ones.</b> They are harder to read than the clear month-year stamps, they wear off easily, and the data behind them is still being pieced together. If a green code is faint or does not add up, lean on the pot codes to pin the year.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 id="wind-down">Where Heel Dating Trails Off</h3>
        <p>Heel dating doesn't stop on a date you can point to, it just gets less reliable. Consistency falls off sharply after 1973, and through the middle of the decade the marking wanders: the green eight-digit codes give way to red ink stamps, and to printed stickers stuck on the heel or in the neck pocket rather than anything written on the wood. Around 1976 the serial number moves to the headstock decal, and after that the heel stops being where a Fender gets dated.</p>
        <p>So treat the mid to late 1970s as the point where this method runs out. On a guitar from that stretch, expect the heel to be inconsistent, and date it from the headstock serial, the pot codes, and the body instead.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- MODEL CODES -->
    <section id="models" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reference</span>
        <h2>Neck Stamp Model Codes</h2>
        <p>The first one or two digits of an ink stamp or a green code are the model number. Here is what those numbers mean. The same model can carry different codes across the years, which is a dating clue in itself.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>Fender renumbered the models at the end of 1965.</b> This is the part that trips people up, and it is worth more than any single code in the table. A model does not keep one number for the whole era. The Stratocaster runs 2 through 1965, then 13 from 1966 to 1968, then 22 from 1968 to about 1973. The Jaguar runs 1 through 1965, then 15 from 1966. So a 1965 Strat heel reads 2MAY65B and a 1966 one reads 13MAY66B, and the guitar in front of you has not changed model, only the year has. If you are working from an old code list that stops at 1965, it will tell you a 1966 Stratocaster is something it is not.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tablewrap compact"><table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">Model</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{modelRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Code" class="code">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="Model">{r[1]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="note-sm">These come from CBS-era neck stamps, and Fender was not always consistent. The codes overlapped and shifted over the years, so read the model prefix next to the other dating clues, not on its own. Gaps in the run are models Fender never assigned a number, or codes not yet documented.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- REISSUES -->
    <section id="reissue" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Watch Out</span>
        <h2>The 1980s Reissues Have Heel Dates Too</h2>
        <p>In 1982 Fender launched the American Vintage Reissue series, US-made copies of the 1950s and 1960s classics, and it brought the heel date back. The Fullerton reissues, 1982 until the plant closed at the end of 1984, carry a graphite pencil date written numerically, like 12-15-83. Documented examples run from 5-11-82 onward, and pencil stayed the method until about 1988, when Fender moved to a black ink stamp using three-letter months.</p>
        <p>There is a catch that sends people the wrong way here. Many Fullerton heels also carry a black ink stamp of the builder or inspector name, and some carry the word VINTAGE stamped on. Neither of those is the date, and it's easy to see one, call the neck ink-stamped, and date it to the wrong end of the 1980s. The pencil date is the date. Some 1982 necks left the factory with no date on them at all, which is its own known quirk rather than a sign of a problem.</p>
        <p>Either way, a reissue date does not prove a guitar is pre-CBS.</p>
        <p>The giveaway is in the detail. A reissue date often includes the day, not just the month and year, and the rest of the guitar, the serial number, the pot codes, and the hardware, will point to the 1980s or later. Read the heel date, then confirm the era with everything else.</p>
        <div class="figrow single">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("1986-fender-avri-strat-pencil-neck-heel.jpg")} alt="The neck heel of a 1986 Fender American Vintage Reissue Stratocaster with a pencil date reading 4-24-86" width={1280} /><figcaption>A 1986 American Vintage Reissue Stratocaster, penciled 4-24-86 for April 24, 1986. Pencil had been the reissue method since the series launched in 1982, and carried on past the move to Corona.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- GOLDEN RULE -->
    <section id="golden">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The One Rule That Matters</span>
        <h2>The Neck Date Dates The Neck</h2>
        <p>Here's the rule every appraiser works by. The neck date tells you when the neck was made, not when the guitar was assembled. Fender built necks in batches and pulled them from a bin, so a neck can predate the finished guitar by weeks or months. On an original, unmodified Fender the neck date and the serial number sit within a few months of each other.</p>
        <p>When they don't line up, there are a few honest explanations. A neck dated a year or two before the body is often a leftover from an earlier run, which is common and completely original. A neck dated well after the rest of the guitar is the red flag, because that usually means the neck was replaced. A documented transitional build, where an older neck went onto a newer body at the factory, is still all original. The paperwork just makes it interesting, not suspicious.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- LIMITS -->
    <section id="limits" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Keep It Honest</span>
        <h2>What A Neck Date Can't Prove</h2>
        <p>A neck date dates the neck and nothing else. It only speaks for the guitar when the neck is original, so make sure the neck date, the body date, and the serial all tell a consistent story. A neck date that sits years off from everything else points to a swapped neck.</p>
        <p>A correct neck date also doesn't prove a guitar is all original. That's a whole-instrument call that weighs the body date, the pot codes, the pickups, the hardware, and the finish together. Treat the neck date as your strongest single clue, then line it up with everything else.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- CROSS-CHECK -->
    <section id="cross">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Put It All Together</span>
        <h2>Cross-Check With The Other Methods</h2>
        <p>The neck date is strongest next to the other clues. Pair it with the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pot-codes/">pot codes</a> for the single best two-part check on a vintage Fender, then confirm with the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-body-dates/">body date</a> in the neck pocket and the serial number. On an original guitar the neck date, the body date, and the serial all land within a few months of each other.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">This guide is one of the dating methods behind the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a>, where the complete year-by-year serial charts live.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Expert Questions</span>
        <h2>Common Questions About Fender Neck Dates</h2>
        <div class="faqs">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="faq">
              <summary>{f.q}</summary>
              <div class="faqbody"><p>{f.a}</p></div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- APPRAISAL -->
    <section>
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="appraise" id="appraisal">
          <span class="eyebrow">Free Expert Appraisal</span>
          <h2>Not Sure What Your Neck Date Is Telling You?</h2>
          <p>A neck can outlive the guitar it started on, and a swapped neck can throw off the whole read. If you want a definite build date, send Joe three clear photos, the front, the back, and the neck heel, for a free, expert appraisal within 24 hours.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="call" href={hrefs.tel}>Call Or Text Joe: {contact.phone}</a>
            <a class="send" href="/free-appraisal/">Or Send Photos Online</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BIO -->
    <section class="pt0">
      <div class="measure"><div class="bio" id="about-joe">
        <LicensedImage src={meta("about-me-joe-1.jpg")} alt="Joe Dampt, owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars, holding a vintage guitar" width={150} />
        <div>
          <div class="who"><a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a></div>
          <div class="cred">Owner, Joe's Vintage Guitars · Mesa, AZ · 12 years full-time · 10,000+ appraised · 2,100+ five-star reviews</div>
          <p class="biotext">Joe Dampt is the owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars in Mesa, Arizona, where he's spent more than twelve years buying, appraising, and authenticating vintage instruments full-time. He's personally valued over 10,000 guitars for sellers in all 50 states, served as a traveling appraiser for the Treasure Seekers Roadshow, and sourced instruments for Grammy Award-winning artists. Every method on this page comes from that bench work.</p>
        </div>
      </div></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- Floating TOC (sidebar + mobile FAB + sheet), matches the hub pattern -->
  <aside class="fspot-toc" id="fspot-toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <p class="fspot-toc__label">On This Page</p>
    <ul class="fspot-toc__list">
      {tocItems.map(([href, label]) => (<li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>))}
    </ul>
  </aside>
  <button class="fspot-toc-fab" id="fspot-toc-fab" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="fspot-toc-sheet"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h16"></path></svg>Jump To A Section</button>
  <div class="fspot-toc-sheet" id="fspot-toc-sheet" role="dialog" aria-label="Jump to a section" aria-modal="false">
    <div class="fspot-toc-sheet__head"><span>Jump To A Section</span><button class="fspot-toc-sheet__close" id="fspot-toc-sheet-close" type="button" aria-label="Close section list">&times;</button></div>
    <div class="fspot-toc-sheet__body" id="fspot-toc-sheet-body"></div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .fspot-preview-note{background:var(--color-brand-charcoal,#2a2d33);color:#f4efe4;font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;text-align:center;padding:.5rem 1rem;position:relative;z-index:3;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop{position:relative;isolation:isolate;z-index:0;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop__dark{background:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);height:54px;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop__cream{background:#fff;height:56px;border-bottom:6px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan,#d2b48c);}

  .fspot{
    --paper:#f5efe3; --panel:#ffffff; --panel-2:#f3ead6; --inset:#fffdf8;
    --ink:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14); --ink-strong:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);
    --muted:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#7a6a54); --line:#ddd0b6;
    --gold:var(--color-brand-gold,#c8983a); --gold-deep:#8c6a24;
    --rust:var(--color-brand-rust,#a03a1e); --rust-bright:var(--color-brand-rust-bright,#be4b25);
    --amber:#f3e3c2; --amber-line:#e3c88a; --darkbrown:#3a2a18; --darkbrown-2:#4a3520;
    --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(58,40,23,.06), 0 10px 30px rgba(58,40,23,.08);
    --fd:var(--font-display), "Oswald", system-ui, sans-serif;
    --fm:ui-monospace,"SF Mono",Consolas,"Liberation Mono",monospace;
    --measure:min(92vw,820px);
    background:var(--paper); color:var(--ink); font-size:17px; line-height:1.6;
    padding-bottom:1rem;
  }
  .fspot [id]{scroll-margin-top:90px;}
  .fspot h1,.fspot h2,.fspot h3{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;line-height:1.12;margin:0;color:var(--ink-strong);text-wrap:balance;}
  .fspot h1{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.6vw,2.9rem);letter-spacing:.005em;text-transform:uppercase;}
  .fspot h2{font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3.1vw,2rem);letter-spacing:.01em;}
  .fspot h3{font-size:clamp(1.21rem,2.1vw,1.34rem);letter-spacing:.015em;margin-top:.8rem;color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot p{margin:0;}
  .fspot a{color:var(--gold-deep);text-underline-offset:2px;}
  .fspot a:hover{color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot .measure{width:var(--measure);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot section{padding-block:clamp(1.7rem,4vw,2.6rem);}
  .fspot .pt0{padding-top:0;}
  .fspot .rule{height:1px;background:var(--line);width:min(94vw,1080px);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot .stack{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .note-sm{font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .eyebrow{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.6em;}
  .fspot .eyebrow::before{content:"";width:24px;height:3px;background:var(--gold);}
  .fspot a.spoke{color:var(--gold-deep);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1.5px dotted var(--gold);font-weight:600;}
  .fspot a.spoke:hover{color:var(--rust);border-bottom-color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot a.back{position:relative;z-index:31;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:.78rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;}
  .fspot a.back::before{content:"\2190";font-size:1rem;}
  .fspot a.back:hover{color:var(--rust-bright);}

  .fspot .hero{padding-top:clamp(1.6rem,4vw,2.6rem);padding-bottom:1.4rem;}
  .fspot .lead{font-size:1.16rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.2rem;}
  .fspot .trust{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.4rem .9rem;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.76rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .trust span{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;}
  .fspot .trust span::before{content:"";width:5px;height:5px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--rust);}

  .fspot .secintro{font-size:.97rem;max-width:70ch;color:var(--ink);}
  .fspot .secintro b{color:var(--ink-strong);}

  .fspot .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;min-width:520px;background:var(--panel);}
  .fspot .tablewrap.compact table{min-width:0;}
  .fspot th,.fspot td{text-align:left;padding:.6rem 1rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top;font-size:.94rem;}
  .fspot thead th{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.65rem;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);background:var(--panel-2);}
  .fspot tbody tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0;}
  .fspot tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--panel-2) 45%,transparent);}
  .fspot .code{font-family:var(--fm);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink);white-space:nowrap;}
  .fspot .yr{font-family:var(--fm);color:var(--rust);font-weight:600;}

  .fspot .figrow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .figrow.single{grid-template-columns:minmax(230px,680px);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot .figrow.single.portrait{grid-template-columns:minmax(230px,500px);}
  .fspot .figrow.gallery{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.4rem;max-width:680px;margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot figure.sfig{margin:0;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--panel);box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot figure.sfig img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;background:var(--panel-2);}
  .fspot figure.sfig figcaption{padding:.55rem .8rem;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted);border-top:1px solid var(--line);}
  .fspot .herofig{margin-top:.3rem;}
  .fspot .herofig figcaption{font-size:.82rem;color:var(--ink);}

  .fspot .videowrap{border:1px solid rgba(200,152,58,.35);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:#000;line-height:0;}
  .fspot .videowrap lite-youtube{max-width:none;width:100%;}
  .fspot .tip{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--darkbrown-2),var(--darkbrown));color:#f0e1c1;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.15rem;font-size:.95rem;}
  .fspot .tip::before{content:"JOE'S TIP";display:block;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--gold);font-size:.78rem;margin-bottom:.4rem;}
  .fspot .tip b{color:#e0be79;}
  .fspot .warnbox{background:#fbe9e6;border:1px solid #e4a99d;border-left:6px solid var(--rust);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.2rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .warnbox__head{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);font-size:.85rem;margin-bottom:.4rem;}
  .fspot .warnbox__head svg{flex:0 0 auto;}
  .fspot .warnbox p{margin:0;color:var(--ink);font-size:.97rem;}

  .fspot .faqs{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem;}
  .fspot details.faq{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);overflow:hidden;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot details.faq>summary{cursor:pointer;list-style:none;padding:.85rem 1.05rem;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:1rem;color:var(--ink-strong);display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:.8rem;}
  .fspot details.faq>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none;}
  .fspot details.faq>summary::after{content:"+";font-family:var(--fm);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--rust);flex:0 0 auto;}
  .fspot details.faq[open]>summary::after{content:"\2212";}
  .fspot details.faq .faqbody{padding:0 1.05rem 1rem;font-size:.96rem;color:var(--muted);}

  .fspot .appraise{background:var(--amber);border:1px solid var(--amber-line);border-left:5px solid var(--rust);border-radius:12px;padding:clamp(1.4rem,3.5vw,2rem);box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .appraise h2{margin-bottom:.5rem;}
  .fspot .appraise p{color:var(--ink);font-size:1.02rem;}
  .fspot .appraise .row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.7rem;margin-top:1.1rem;align-items:center;}
  .fspot .appraise .call{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;letter-spacing:.02em;color:#fff;background:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;padding:.7rem 1.3rem;border-radius:8px;}
  .fspot .appraise .call:hover{background:var(--rust-bright);}
  .fspot .appraise .send{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:.86rem;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);}

  .fspot .bio{display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:center;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;background:var(--panel);}
  .fspot .bio img{flex:0 0 auto;width:76px;height:76px;border-radius:50%;object-fit:cover;border:2px solid var(--gold);}
  .fspot .bio .who{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:1.2rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.02em;}
  .fspot .bio .who a{color:var(--ink-strong);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);}
  .fspot .bio .cred{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.2rem;}
  .fspot .bio .biotext{margin-top:.55rem;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}

  /* mobile card tables */
  @media (max-width:719px){
    .fspot .tablewrap{overflow:visible;border:0;border-radius:0;box-shadow:none;}
    .fspot .tablewrap table{min-width:0;width:100%;display:block;background:transparent;}
    .fspot .tablewrap thead{display:none;}
    .fspot .tablewrap tbody{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.65rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap tr{display:block;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);padding:.7rem .85rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
    .fspot .tablewrap tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:var(--panel);}
    .fspot .tablewrap td{display:block;border:0;padding:.25rem 0;font-size:.95rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap td::before{content:attr(data-label);display:block;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.1rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap .code,.fspot .tablewrap .yr{white-space:normal;}
  }

  /* floating TOC */
  .fspot-toc{display:none;z-index:25;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan,#d2b48c);border-radius:10px;width:260px;max-height:calc(100vh - 160px);padding:16px 18px;position:fixed;top:120px;right:24px;overflow-y:auto;box-shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.08);}
  @media (min-width:1440px){.fspot-toc{display:block;}}
  .fspot-toc__label{font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.13em;color:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);border-bottom:1px solid #a03a1e2e;margin:0 0 10px;padding-bottom:8px;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;}
  .fspot-toc__list{margin:0;padding:0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.4;list-style:none;}
  .fspot-toc__list li{margin:0;}
  .fspot-toc__list a{color:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#6c5238);border-radius:4px;padding:5px 6px;text-decoration:none;display:block;}
  .fspot-toc__list a:hover{background:var(--color-brand-cream-warm,#f5ead4);color:var(--color-brand-rust,#a03a1e);}
  .fspot-toc-fab{display:none;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet{display:none;}
  @media (max-width:1439.98px){
    .fspot-toc-fab{z-index:45;background:var(--color-brand-rust-bright,#be4b25);color:#fff;font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;cursor:pointer;border:none;border-radius:999px;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:11px 18px;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;display:inline-flex;position:fixed;bottom:1.5rem;left:16px;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.28);}
    .fspot-toc-fab svg{width:18px;height:18px;}
    .fspot-toc-fab[aria-expanded="true"]{background:var(--color-brand-rust-dark,#682412);}
    .fspot-toc-sheet{z-index:46;opacity:0;visibility:hidden;background:#fff;border:1px solid #a03a1e33;border-radius:12px;flex-direction:column;width:min(340px,100vw - 32px);max-height:60vh;transition:opacity .2s,transform .2s,visibility .2s;display:flex;position:fixed;bottom:5rem;left:16px;overflow-y:auto;transform:translateY(12px);box-shadow:0 10px 34px rgba(0,0,0,.28);}
    .fspot-toc-sheet.fspot-toc-sheet--open{opacity:1;visibility:visible;transform:translateY(0);}
  }
  .fspot-toc-sheet__head{font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-brand-rust-dark,#682412);background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #a03a1e26;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;display:flex;position:sticky;top:0;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__close{color:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#6c5238);cursor:pointer;background:none;border:none;padding:0 4px;font-size:24px;line-height:1;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body{padding:8px 10px;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body .fspot-toc__list{font-size:14px;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body .fspot-toc__list a{padding:8px 6px;}
</style>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Floating TOC: clone list into the mobile sheet, wire the FAB
    var list = document.querySelector('#fspot-toc .fspot-toc__list');
    var body = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet-body');
    if (list && body) body.appendChild(list.cloneNode(true));
    var fab = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-fab'),
        sheet = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet'),
        closeBtn = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet-close');
    function setOpen(o) { if (!sheet) return; sheet.classList.toggle('fspot-toc-sheet--open', o); if (fab) fab.setAttribute('aria-expanded', o ? 'true' : 'false'); }
    if (fab) fab.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!sheet.classList.contains('fspot-toc-sheet--open')); });
    if (closeBtn) closeBtn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) { if (sheet && fab && !sheet.contains(e.target) && !fab.contains(e.target)) setOpen(false); });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
    if (sheet) sheet.addEventListener('click', function (e) { if (e.target.closest('a')) setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
